  "query_preview": "/THURSDAY THROUGH MONDAY/...\nIssued at 242 AM MDT Tue Sep 29 2026\n\nMuch quieter weather returns for the second half of the week as \n250mb flow turns northwesterly to westerly behind the departing \ntrough. Northwesterly flow on Thursday will keep temperatures cool \nacross the CWA with highs currently forecast to be in the 60s \neverywhere. Little to not precipitation is anticipated on Thursday \nas subsident flow remains in place. A warming trend will start \nFriday and continue through the weekend as a ridge attempts to build \nacross the western CONUS. Temperatures will warm into the upper-60s \nto mid-70s with no precipitation expected as the moisture plume from \nthe Pacific Ocean remains cutoff. Upper-level ridging will continue \nto build into the weekend as a trough digs southward just off the \ncoast of California. This system could be the next precipitation \nproducing to impact the region, but for the remainder of the long \nterm forecast period it remains well off to the west. Temperatures \non Saturday will warm into the upper-60s to mid-70s with \ntemperatures on Sunday following suit, in the upper-60s to mid-70s. \nTemperatures start to warm more Monday onwards as an upper-level \nridge builds ahead of the incoming trough off the coast of \nCalifornia. Highs on Monday are currently forecast to be in the mid-\n70s to low-80s, with slightly warmer temperatures possible on \nTuesday, depending on the system's track.\n\nThe system off the coast of California could start to impact the \nregion as early as Monday morning, with long range models currently \nsuggesting a brisk eastward movement through the weekend. The GFS \nand ECMWF are in fairly good agreement on this system with the 00Z \nruns, though the GFS positions the trough slightly further north \nthan the ECMWF. Models disagreement starts to increase into Tuesday \nmorning, with the GFS suggesting a more progressive trough while the \nECMWF slows the trough down and keeps it much further west. Long \nrange models suggest precipitation chances starting to return to \nstart the work week, with low-end PoPs over western portions of the \nCWA as soon as Monday afternoon.",
